Problem:
A nutritionist recommends that a child eat fruits and drink juice to tackle a certain disease. Each serving of fruit contains 5 units of the necessary mineral and 3 units of hydration. Each serving of juice contains 7 units of the necessary mineral and 6 units of hydration. According to the nutritionist, the child must intake at least 60 units of the necessary mineral every week and 40 units of hydration for their body to better absorb the mineral into their body. Further, each serving of fruit contains 9 units of sugar and each serving of juice contains 14 units of sugar. Since juice is more convenient, the child wants to take at least twice as many servings of juice as fruits. How many of each should the child be served to minimize the total sugar intake?

